{{also|Indicator}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*deyḱ-}}
From {{der|en|LL.|indicātor||one who points out}}, from {{der|en|la|indicō||point out}}; see [[indicate]].  {{doublet|en|indexer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɪn.dɪ.ˌkeɪ.tə(ɹ)/}}
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 indicator.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[pointer]] or [[index]] that [[indicate]]s something.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Andrew Martin|title=Underground Overground: A passenger's history of the Tube|publisher=Profile Books, {{ISBN|978-1846684777}}|page=79|text=These old '''indicators''' <small>[at Earl's Court station]</small> show the destination of the next train by the appearance of an illuminated arrow next to the station name. The arrow may indicate that a train is going to [[Wimbledon]], but it gives no clue as to ''when''. Now, however, the '''indicators''' are supplemented by dot matrix panels that not only say where but also when. Why aren't the old ones removed? Because they, like the whole station, are Grade II listed.}}
# A [[meter]] or [[gauge]].
# The [[needle]] or [[dial]] on such a meter.
# {{lb|en|chemistry}} Any of many substances, such as [[litmus]], used to indicate the [[concentration]] of a [[substance]], or the [[degree]] of a [[reaction]].
# {{lb|en|ecology}} A [[plant]] or [[animal]] whose [[presence]] is [[indicative]] of some specific [[environment]].
# {{lb|en|economics}} A [[measure]], such as [[unemployment]] [[rate]], which can be used to [[predict]] [[economic]] [[trend]]s.
# {{lb|en|UK|Australia|New Zealand|automotive}} A [[turn signal]]; each of the flashing lights on each side of a vehicle which indicate a turn is being made to left or right, or a lane change etc.
#: {{syn|en|blinker|q1=informal|directional|directional signal|direction indicator|trafficator|turn indicator|turn signal|q7=chiefly US}}
# A bird, the [[honeyguide]].

